  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe Great Lakes and Ohio Valley remained locked into an unsettled \ndeformation pattern again today, which steered yet another MCS \nacross the Ohio Valley. A lackluster showing locally with just a \nbrief flare up in shower activity near the state line already \ndecaying at issuance. That said, composite reflectivity does show \noutflow emanating north from the complex which will be one of \nseveral small-scale forcing mechanisms capable of triggering \nadditional convection this evening. \n\nStable but moist low levels characterize the airmass across the \nsouthern counties where surface dewpoints are near 70 degrees. \nDelineation in cloud cover throughout the day has set up an \nimpressive differential heating boundary around the I-94 corridor. \nTemperatures underneath the stratus deck are only in the low 70s \ncompared to low 80s in partial sun to the north. With 6.0-6.5 C/km \nmid level lapse rates present across all of SE Michigan, instability \nis tied to the moistened boundary layer conditions which are most \nfavorable where rain fell earlier today across the Saginaw \nValley/Thumb region and near the Lake Huron shoreline. This also \nhappens to be where the nose of an upper level jet will connect with \nlow level troughing to present a more focused area of weak ascent \nthis evening. \n\nCurrent satellite trends show a few spots where towering cumulus is \nattempting to gain traction, mainly along the lake breeze influence \nfrom Bad Axe south. The existing stable layer is still broadly \npreventing deep convective growth, as has been the case the past \nseveral days, although a couple of deeper cores have developed along \nthe interacting lake breeze boundaries. Expectation is for the \narrival of the synoptic ascent and lake breezes/outflow to piece \ntogether enough ascent for more updrafts to break through the cap. \nThus additional convection development is expected overhead through \nthe evening (roughly 22z-03z or 6pm-11pm EDT). This will mainly \noccur north of the M-59 corridor where instability is most \nfavorable. The deepest updrafts will have access to ~40 knots of 0-\n6km bulk shear and cape density throughout the mid-levels. Isolated \nstorms will thus be capable or 1+ inch hail and downbursts of 60+ \nmph. SWODY1 continues to highlight a Marginal Risk for much of the \narea.  \n\nLow confidence pattern lingers through the rest of the week into the \nweekend as the deformation axis loiters over the Ohio Valley. \nDisjointed moisture and weak forcing will continue to struggle \ngenerating convection, while a southward shift in the boundary \nThursday favors a dry forecast for all of SE Michigan. This boundary \ndoes attempt to lift back north Friday-this weekend, but will be \ncompeting with high pressure over Ontario which keeps better \nmoisture/instability to our south and west. Upstream convection may \nstill have an impact on cloud cover, while residual low level \nmoisture affords fog chances again Thursday morning--both \ninfluencing temperatures. \n\nRelief from this unsettled pattern finally arrives late this weekend-\nearly next week as low pressure ejects out of the northern Rockies \ninto Canada. This drives a cold front across the Upper Midwest and \nGreat Lakes which brings an increased chance of showers and storms \nthis weekend, but a bout of high pressure behind it. Seasonable \ntemperatures in the low-mid 80s are expected throughout the 7-day \nforecast period.",
